> Derive interpolation and truncation

Assume finite opposite nonzero endpoint values, a<b, continuity and trustworthy
function signs. The line through the endpoints crosses zero at

$$
x_f=\frac{f_ba-f_ab}{f_b-f_a}
=a+(b-a)\frac{|f_a|}{|f_a|+|f_b|}.
$$
\alttext(The regula-falsi crossing is a convex interpolation of the bracket endpoints weighted by their function magnitudes.)

The implementation scales the magnitudes before forming their ratio, then uses
wide arithmetic for positions. This avoids double cross-products and the sum of
huge callback magnitudes. It changes arithmetic ordering from the old formula,
so last digits or trial counts are not promised identical at rounded boundaries.

Let m be the bracket centre and w its width. This implementation fixes kappa2=2
and kappa1=0.2 divided by the initial width. Truncation moves toward the centre
without overshooting it:

$$
\delta=0.2\frac{w^2}{w_0},\qquad
x_t=x_f+\operatorname{sign}(m-x_f)\min(\delta,|m-x_f|).
$$
\alttext(Truncation moves the secant estimate toward the midpoint by at most delta and never crosses the midpoint.)

The code evaluates delta as 0.2*w*(w/w0), avoiding an unnecessary square and
reciprocal at extreme scales. This is a fixed scaled parameter policy, not a
general interface to every parameter choice studied in the literature.

> Derive the projection bound and the allowance

For positive coordinate tolerance epsilon, define the nonnegative bisection
halving count and the allowed projection count:

$$
N_{1/2}=\max\left(0,\left\lceil\log_2\frac{w_0}{2\epsilon}\right\rceil\right),
\qquad N=N_{1/2}+n_0.
$$
\alttext(The exact-arithmetic ITP trial allowance is the bisection halving bound plus nonnegative n0.)

The code obtains the first count by checked repeated halving, avoiding a
logarithm followed by an unchecked conversion to int. At zero-based trial j,
project the truncated estimate into the feasible band around the centre:

$$
r_j=\epsilon2^{N-j}-w_j/2,\qquad
x_{ITP}=\operatorname{clamp}(x_t,m-r_j,m+r_j).
$$
\alttext(The projected estimate stays within a radius that leaves enough reductions to meet the remaining width budget.)

Intersecting that band with the bracket is equivalent to capping its radius at
half-width. In exact arithmetic, either retained child has width at most
w/2+abs(xITP−m), which is at most epsilon times 2 to the power N−j. Inductively,
the radius stays nonnegative and the bracket after N trials is no wider than two
epsilon. With n0=0 this is bisection's worst-case halving bound; the default
n0=1 permits one extra trial. It does not promise fewer calls than bisection on
every function: bisection may hit a callback zero immediately.

Finite precision adds qualifications. The code rounds the actual coordinate to
double, falls back to an interior midpoint if rounding hits an endpoint, and
marks trials whose rounding required adjustment or left the wide projection
band. If no interior midpoint exists, it reports stagnation. If the computed
allowance expires before the actual returned midpoint radius passes tolerance,
it reports projection limit with converged=false. These are numerical outcomes,
not certified directed-rounding interval bounds.

The
[maintained ITP package documentation|https://paulnorthrop.github.io/itp/reference/itp.html]
records the n0 allowance and separate width criterion. Its
[reference implementation|https://github.com/paulnorthrop/itp/blob/master/src/itp.cpp]
shows the three stages. The original method is Oliveira and Takahashi,
[ACM TOMS47(1), article5, 2021|https://doi.org/10.1145/3423597]. This report
derives the width bound above rather than asserting an unconditional superlinear
rate. Local rate claims require smoothness/simple-root and parameter
assumptions, and are not inferred from one plotted trajectory.

> Retain the original cosine fixed point and match the stopping controls

The original problem is cos(x)−x on [0,1], with root approximately
0.7390851332151607. Its summary used combined tolerance 1e-12, while its
report-only comparison loops used position tolerance 1e-12 and residual
threshold 1e-13. We preserve those as two explicitly different experiments. The
comparison now calls the canonical ITP and bisection traces with the same two
thresholds. All candidate values come from their producing loops; there are no
hand-coded solver loops or repeated-prefix reruns. The horizontal axis counts
actual callbacks, including the two endpoints, rather than comparing unlike
iteration units.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/itp_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/itp_analysis.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <limits>
#include <cmath>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto f = [](double x) { return std::cos(x) - x; };
    const auto summary = dm::algorithms::itp_run(f, 0, 1, {1e-12, 1e-12});
    const auto a = dm::algorithms::itp_trace(f, 0, 1, {1e-12, 1e-13});
    const auto b =
        dm::algorithms::bisection_trace(f, 0, 1, {1e-12, 1e-13, 100});
    std::print("| experiment | root | residual | projection or midpoint trials | callback calls | stop |\n| " "--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    std::print("| original combined summary | {:.15f} | {:.8e} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
               summary.result.root, summary.result.residual,
               summary.result.iterations, summary.evaluations,
               dm::algorithms::itp_stop_name(summary.stop));
    std::print("| matched ITP comparison | {:.15f} | {:.8e} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
               a.run.result.root, a.run.result.residual,
               a.run.result.iterations, a.run.evaluations,
               dm::algorithms::itp_stop_name(a.run.stop));
    std::print("| matched bisection comparison | {:.15f} | {:.8e} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
               b.result.root, b.result.residual, b.result.iterations,
               b.evaluations, dm::algorithms::bisection_stop_name(b.stop));
    dm::algorithms::itp_bisection_plot(a, b, true, 1e-16).title(
        "Original cosine problem: actual matched residual histories").ylim(
        -17, 2).save_svg("build/itp_original.svg");
}
