\caption(The discontinuity satisfies the numerical interval test without a root. Equal endpoint magnitudes make ITP choose the midpoint, so the two curves coincide. The precision controls return converged=false.)
\alttext(Two panels show a shrinking finite-jump bracket above and constant unit residual below.)

> Counts, ownership and numerical scope

Each actual projection trial evaluates f once. Initial endpoint evaluation stops
immediately if the first endpoint callback is zero; otherwise both endpoints are
evaluated. A terminal midpoint is an additional callback when the width, caller
budget, projection budget or precision stops the trial loop. It does not
increment the projection-trial count. Endpoint calls are not step records; every
actual projected or terminal-midpoint candidate call has a scalar snapshot. The
stable stopping reason identifies which criterion actually passed or failed. A
callback zero is a floating computation, not proof of an exact mathematical
zero. Residual acceptance is strict; coordinate acceptance is non-strict for the
actual returned midpoint radius.

Time is O(K plus the initial halving-count work), plus at most K+3 callback
calls, for K permitted projection trials. Untraced runs use constant auxiliary
storage; traces own O(K) scalar records. There is no derivative evaluation,
function replay, prefix rerun or retained callable. Callback exceptions
propagate and their already-executed side effects persist. Independent calls
share no mutable library state.

Finite ordered/reversed endpoints and positive finite coordinate tolerance are
validated, as are nonnegative finite residual tolerance, trial budget and n0.
The n0 addition is checked before callbacks. Nonfinite callback values throw;
callback-sign comparisons do not multiply values. Working arithmetic uses long
double, with platform-dependent range/precision; unrepresentable arithmetic
throws. Large slack exponents are handled without overflowing a needless power.
No arbitrary smoothness, monotonicity, continuity or evaluation-accuracy
property can be inferred from finite callback values.

The default online history cap is one million scalar candidate records, admitted
before each recorded candidate callback; zero disables it, and untraced runs
ignore it. No partial history returns on an error. This excludes endpoint calls,
vector capacity, callback-owned storage and byte/peak RAM. Graphics apply a
separate complete combined point cap before building plot data, validate the
shown scalars and callback-count contract, and do not establish the provenance
of an edited history or verify that two caller-supplied callbacks were
identical. Figures explicitly transform values with log10(max(value,declared
floor)); a floor point is a display choice, not additional accuracy.

The legacy itp signature and RootResult layout remain available. It delegates to
the same kernel with tol applied in both coordinate and function units, n0=1 and
no recording. Zero trial budget still evaluates endpoints and a final midpoint
where needed; invalid negative budgets now throw. Unlike the old code, invalid
logarithm/int conversions, nonfinite evaluations and rounded precision limits
cannot silently claim an ordinary successful solve.

> Verification

Twelve selected native cases pass AddressSanitizer and
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, including 2432 generated sign-transition brackets,
all recorded stage and bracket updates, exact callback sequences, separate-unit
controls, endpoint/no-bracket/ zero-budget outcomes, extreme scales, cap
admission, ownership, graphics and an explicit rounded-projection failure. Three
existing scalar-root test cases also pass within that selected suite.
Independent 90-digit Decimal checks cover 623 runs, 12546 actual projection
trials and 615 actual final-midpoint calls, verifying every stage, callback,
bracket update, budget and stopping outcome, including one rounded
projection-band departure and limit failure. The frozen original solver agrees
on 1003 finite continuous controls within 2.23e-16 in returned coordinate; those
controls have unchanged trial counts. This is a bounded compatibility check, not
a promise of identical arithmetic everywhere.

All 59 module/helper definitions are documented, eleven public examples compile
and execute, and four representative clangd hovers pass. These checks concern
the fixed scaled-parameter implementation and the displayed experiments, not a
certification of arbitrary black-box functions or the historical report corpus.
